        "bullets": [
            "Gulf News reports three vessels attacked in 72 hours as the maritime threat level at the Strait of Hormuz remains severe.",
            "PortWatch recorded 1 transit on 2026-08-09, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports reached 780 in the most recent 24 hours.",
            "The Crisis Pressure index holds at extreme while the Escalation Forecast sits at elevated and rising, driven by Polymarket escalation contracts."
        ],
        "date": "2026-08-17",
        "generatedAt": "2026-08-17T11:53:23.217Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "Operational conditions at the Strait of Hormuz remain severely disrupted. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-08-09, recorded 1 transit against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that figure is eight days old given PortWatch's weekly Tuesday publication cycle, but it frames the scale of the deviation. Separately, our AIS tracking shows 780 port arrivals across five Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides in the most recent 24 hours, and 347 vessels holding position away from berth as of 11:53 UTC, a count that excludes ships within 25 km of a working port. Gulf News reports three vessels were attacked in the past 72 hours, with the maritime threat level described as severe; that account is single-source and not independently confirmed. Per Iranian state media, not independently confirmed, shipping through the strait slowed to near zero over the weekend. Middle East Eye reports Iran and Oman are working on a temporary alternative shipping route as pressure from Washington mounts, though the arrangement remains conditional. Brent crude stood at $88.90, down 0.09% over the prior 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC. The site's Crisis Pressure composite holds at extreme, with physical transit deviation as the primary driver; the Escalation Forecast sits at elevated and is rising, with Polymarket escalation contracts the top contributor. The two indices diverge, signaling that current operational stress has not yet translated into a forecast of imminent further escalation, though the forecast trend is moving in that direction.",
